      ["A scheduled basal-bolus-correction insulin regimen (basal insulin once daily plus prandial rapid-acting insulin before meals and correction scale)", "The patient is a hospitalized medical inpatient with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes. According to ADA and Endocrine Society guidelines and the landmark RABBIT-2 randomized controlled trial, SLIDING-SCALE INSULIN ALONE AS MONOTHERAPY IS STRONGLY DISCOURAGED AND INEFFECTIVE. Sliding-scale monotherapy is reactive rather than proactive: it treats hyperglycemia only AFTER it occurs, fails to provide continuous baseline insulin, and causes severe glycemic volatility ('roller-coaster' peaks and troughs) and increased infection rates. The established standard of care for non-critically ill hospitalized patients with consistent nutritional intake is a SCHEDULED BASAL-BOLUS-CORRECTION REGIMEN: (1) Basal insulin (glargine, detemir, or degludec) to suppress baseline hepatic gluconeogenesis; (2) Nutritional/prandial insulin (lispro, aspart, or glulisine) given before meals to cover mealtime carbohydrate excursions; and (3) Correction scale insulin administered concurrently with prandial doses to correct pre-meal hyperglycemia. Basal-bolus regimens achieve significantly superior glycemic control, faster resolution of hyperglycemia, and lower rates of hospital complications without increasing severe hypoglycemia compared to sliding-scale monotherapy."],
