      ["Maintain the transfusion cessation, disconnect the blood tubing, infuse intravenous 0.9% normal saline aggressively to maintain urine output > 100 to 200 mL/hr, and send the blood unit, tubing, and fresh patient blood/urine samples to the blood bank", "The patient is experiencing an ACUTE HEMOLYTIC TRANSFUSION REACTION (AHTR), a life-threatening medical emergency caused by ABO incompatibility (most commonly human clerical error / misidentification). Preformed host IgM antibodies (anti-A in a Type O recipient) bind to transfused donor red blood cells, activating the classical complement cascade to completion (membrane attack complex C5b-9), causing massive INTRAVASCULAR HEMOLYSIS, shock, disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), and acute kidney injury (ATN from free hemoglobin-induced renal tubular obstruction and vasoconstriction). THE MANDATORY RESUSCITATION PROTOCOL: (1) STOP THE TRANSFUSION IMMEDIATELY and disconnect all blood tubing from the IV catheter (do not flush remaining blood in the line); (2) MAINTAIN IV ACCESS and initiate AGGRESSIVE INTRAVENOUS NORMAL SALINE RESUSCITATION to treat hypotension and maintain a high urine output (> 100–200 mL/hr in adults) to prevent free hemoglobin precipitation in renal tubules and wash out toxic heme pigments; (3) Perform a clerical check and IMMEDIATELY NOTIFY THE BLOOD BANK, sending the implicated blood unit, IV tubing, and freshly drawn patient blood (for repeat typing, crossmatching, direct Coombs test, and free hemoglobin) and urine (for hemoglobinuria); and (4) Monitor coagulation parameters for DIC."],
