      ["Oral fidaxomicin (200 mg twice daily for 10 days) or oral vancomycin (125 mg four times daily for 10 days), strict contact precautions, and mandatory hand hygiene with soap and water", "The patient has an initial episode of non-severe Clostridioides difficile infection (CDI, defined by WBC < 15,000/mm3 and serum creatinine < 1.5 mg/dL). According to the updated IDSA/SHEA clinical practice guidelines: (1) FIRST-LINE ANTIMICROBIAL THERAPY: ORAL FIDAXOMICIN (200 mg twice daily for 10 days) IS NOW THE PREFERRED FIRST-LINE AGENT over oral vancomycin because fidaxomicin is a narrow-spectrum macrocyclic bactericidal antibiotic that selectively targets C. difficile while sparing normal colonic bacteroidetes/firmicutes flora, resulting in significantly lower rates of CDI recurrence. ORAL VANCOMYCIN (125 mg four times daily for 10 days) is the established alternative first-line regimen. (2) OBSOLESCENCE OF METRONIDAZOLE: Oral metronidazole is INFERIOR in clinical cure and recurrence rates and is EXPLICITLY NO LONGER RECOMMENDED for initial CDI if fidaxomicin or vancomycin is available. (3) INFECTION CONTROL: Strict CONTACT PRECAUTIONS (gloves and gowns). MANDATORY HAND HYGIENE WITH SOAP AND WATER IS REQUIRED: alcohol-based hand sanitizers do NOT eradicate bacterial endospores; mechanical friction and washing with soap and water physically removes spores from hands."],
      ["Oral metronidazole 500 mg three times daily for 10 days and hand hygiene using alcohol-based foam sanitizers", "Metronidazole is clinically inferior and no longer recommended by IDSA guidelines, and alcohol rubs do not kill C. difficile spores."],
      ["Intravenous vancomycin 1 g every 12 hours combined with oral loperamide", "Intravenous vancomycin is not secreted into the colonic lumen and is completely ineffective for luminal C. diff; antimotility agents (loperamide) cause toxic megacolon and perforation."],
      ["Oral fidaxomicin combined with immediate fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT)", "Fecal microbiota transplantation is indicated strictly for multiple recurrent CDI (>= 2 recurrences failing vancomycin/fidaxomicin); it is not indicated for an initial episode."],
      ["Intravenous tigecycline monotherapy and transfer to the intensive care unit", "Tigecycline is a third-line rescue agent reserved strictly for fulminant, refractory, life-threatening CDI shock; it is not indicated for non-severe initial infection."]
